    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large language models exceed the memory capacity of a single accelerator, and mixture-of-experts (MoE) models add a second dimension of scale: enormous total parameter counts with sparse per-token activation. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and attention heads) across devices so every device holds a shard of every layer. All devices process the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: partitions the *experts* of an MoE layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the expert that the router selected.\n\nIn inference, the goal is to maximize throughput (tokens/sec) and minimize latency (time-to-first-token, inter-token latency) subject to memory and bandwidth constraints. TP reduces per-device memory and increases raw FLOPs; EP exploits the sparsity of MoE routing so that total expert capacity can grow without a proportional increase in per-token compute. Production systems (e.g., DeepSeek-style deployments, vLLM/SGLang with MoE support, TensorRT-LLM) combine both: attention layers use TP, MoE feed-forward layers use EP, and a data-parallel (DP) outer dimension replicates the whole pipeline for batch scaling.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Model Partitioning\n\n### 2.1 Tensor parallel sharding\n\nFor a transformer layer, the standard Megatron-style split is:\n\n- **Attention**: QKV projection is column-parallel (each device computes a slice of heads); output projection `W_O` is row-parallel (each device holds a slice of columns, producing partial sums).\n- **MLP (dense)**: `W_up`/`W_gate` column-parallel, `W_down` row-parallel.\n- **Embeddings and LM head**: vocabulary",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
